[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Pessimistic vs Optimistic Concurrency Control in Database Design"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When multiple transactions attempt to modify the same data simultaneously in a database management system, concurrency control mechanisms ensure data integrity and prevent race conditions. The two primary strategies for handling this are Pessimistic Concurrency Control (PCC) and Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC)."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Pessimistic Concurrency Control (PCC)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pessimistic concurrency control assumes that conflicts are likely to occur. It prevents conflicts by locking data as soon as it is read and holding the lock until the transaction is fully completed or rolled back. Other transactions attempting to access the locked data must wait."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"How PCC Works"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Read Phase: A transaction requests a shared or exclusive lock on the targeted rows or tables."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Modification: The application reads and modifies the data while the lock remains active."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Release Phase: The transaction commits or aborts, releasing the lock for other waiting transactions."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"PCC SQL Implementation Example"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"BEGIN TRANSACTION;
SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE id = 1 FOR UPDATE;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
COMMIT;"},{"order":11,"type":"heading","text":"Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC)"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Optimistic concurrency control assumes that data conflicts are rare. Instead of locking records upfront, transactions proceed without locking. Data is read, modified, and validated only at the moment of the commit. If another transaction modified the data in the meantime, the current transaction fails and must be retried."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"The Three Phases of OCC"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Read Phase: The transaction reads data, tracks the state using version numbers or timestamps, and performs local updates."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Validation Phase: The system checks if any other transaction has modified the data read during the transaction."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Write Phase: If validation succeeds, changes are written to the database. If it fails, the transaction aborts and restarts."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"OCC Implementation Example using Version Columns"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"-- Step 1: Read data and note the version
SELECT balance, version FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;

-- Step 2: Update ensuring version has not changed
UPDATE accounts 
SET balance = balance - 100, version = version + 1 
WHERE id = 1 AND version = 5;"},{"order":19,"type":"heading","text":"Comparison and Summary"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Pessimistic Concurrency Control when write contention is high, transactions are long-lived, and the cost of aborting and retrying transactions is high."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Optimistic Concurrency Control when read operations heavily outnumber write operations, contention is low, and high throughput is required without locking bottlenecks."}]
